1. How many system calls does each method make?
  Method 1 (System Calls)
      This method makes a system call for every chunk of data
      There’ll be many read lines in trace.txt if the file is large and if the buffer is small
  Method 2 (Library Functions
      This method makes few calls.
      Even if you call fgets() ten times in your code, the library will usually perform only one large read to fill its internal buffer
      
2. What's the difference between fopen() and open()?
  open()
      This is a direct system call 
      It asks the kernel to provide a file descriptor
      It provides no buffering
      It is a raw interface to the filesystem
  fopen()
      This is a library function that wraps around open()
      It creates a FILE object that handles buffering 
      This makes it easier to read data line-by-line or character-by-character
      
3. Why does the library method make different system calls?
  The library method is designed for efficiency
      Switching from "User Mode" to "Kernel Mode" is extensive for the CPU. 
      Reading a large block of data once into a buffer lets the library minimizes the switches
            Easier on the CPU
  
